<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Canton of Zug</h1><p class="st-meta">1352 CE – 1798 CE · capital Zug</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small lakeside canton that joined the Swiss Confederacy in 1352 and remained a Catholic stronghold of central Switzerland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Zug was a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-zug">Lake of Zug</a>, surrounded by rural communities. In 1352 Confederate forces besieged it, and the town and countryside joined the alliance of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich</a> and the forest cantons, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> influence lingered until the Swiss victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Sempach</a> in 1386. Zug counted among the eight old cantons.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Government was unusually shared between the town of Zug and three rural communes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ageri">Ägeri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menzingen">Menzingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baar-switzerland">Baar</a>, which met together in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a> and rotated offices. The economy rested on farming, fishing, trade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> service. Zug remained Catholic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and fought on the Catholic side at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kappel">Kappel</a> in 1531.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The old order ended with the French invasion of 1798 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. Restored as a canton in 1803, Zug remains part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation</a> and is now known as a low-tax business centre.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1352 CE – 1798 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.61%;width:8.88%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1352 CE – 1798 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zug</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swiss German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zug</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1575&amp;focus=canton-of-zug" aria-label="Canton of Zug on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 522 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Canton of Zug lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5200 778L5186 782M5200 778L5188 772"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5186 782h0M5188 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 778h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 778h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zug. <a href="/atlas?year=1575&amp;focus=canton-of-zug">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
